C["articles"]['budget'] = ('da-lat-trip-budget', 'Da Lat Trip Budget: How to Plan Your Costs | Van Phu', 'Planning a Da Lat Trip Budget: What to Expect and How to Save', 'How to plan a Da Lat trip budget: transport, where to stay, food, sights and getting around by scooter — what costs more, what costs less and simple ways to save.', "Da Lat is one of the easier places in Vietnam to travel on a modest budget, but costs add up quickly if you don't plan. Here is a practical way to think about your Da Lat trip budget — what usually costs more, what costs less, and where a rented scooter fits in.", 'honda-vision-girls-helmets-dalat', '\n<h2>The main parts of a Da Lat budget</h2>\n<p>Most travellers spend their money on five things: getting to Da Lat, a place to sleep, food and coffee, entrance tickets, and getting around the city. Prices for hotels, tours and tickets change with the season and from place to place, so instead of guessing exact numbers, plan a range for each part and check current prices when you book.</p>\n<h2>Getting to Da Lat</h2>\n<p>Overnight sleeper buses from Ho Chi Minh City and daytime buses from Nha Trang are usually the cheapest way in, and an overnight bus also saves you one night of accommodation. Flying into Lien Khuong Airport, about 30 km south of the city, is faster but normally costs more, plus the transfer into town. Our guide on <a href="{ART:getting}">how to get to Da Lat</a> compares the options.</p>\n<h2>Where to stay</h2>\n<p>Da Lat has everything from dorm beds and family homestays to boutique villas and big resorts. Accommodation is usually the biggest variable in your budget. Weekends, Vietnamese public holidays, Tết and the Flower Festival period are busier and more expensive, so book early for those dates. Weekdays outside holidays are calmer and often better value. See <a href="{ART:where-to-stay}">where to stay in Da Lat</a> to choose an area.</p>\n<h2>Food and coffee</h2>\n<p>Eating like a local is one of the best ways to keep costs down. Street food, noodle soups, broken rice and bánh mì are inexpensive, and Da Lat\'s cafés are a pleasure in themselves. Restaurants with a view, hotel restaurants and imported dishes cost noticeably more. At the night market, ask the price before you order and agree on it. Ideas in <a href="{ART:food}">what to eat in Da Lat</a>.</p>\n{PHOTO:honda-vision-cafe-garden-dalat}\n<h2>Sights and activities</h2>\n<p>Many of Da Lat\'s best experiences are free or almost free: Xuan Huong Lake, the pine roads, the tea hills at sunrise, the flower villages and the viewpoints along the way. Waterfalls, gardens, cable cars and some attractions charge entrance tickets at the gate, and the price depends on the place. A good budget habit: pick two or three paid sights per day and fill the rest with free rides and walks.</p>\n<h2>Getting around: the scooter part of your budget</h2>\n<p>This is the one cost we can give you exactly. At Van Phu every bike — Honda Vision 110cc, Honda Air Blade 125cc or electric scooter — costs 150,000 VND per day, fixed, with no cash deposit (you leave a passport or ID card). That includes free helmets and a phone holder, and gas scooters come with 1 liter of fuel. A week costs 1,050,000 VND — see <a href="{ART:lt-multiday}">renting for a week or longer</a>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Fuel:</b> filling up a small scooter at a petrol station is cheap, and one tank goes a long way around Da Lat.</li>\n<li><b>Electric scooter:</b> no fuel at all; if you need a fresh battery, we swap it at the shop for 20,000 VND.</li>\n<li><b>Parking:</b> guarded "gửi xe" lots near sights and markets charge a small fee — keep some small notes.</li>\n<li><b>Delivery:</b> for rentals of 2+ days we deliver free to hotels in central Da Lat, which saves a taxi ride.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Even for one traveller, a scooter is usually cheaper than taking taxis or ride-hailing to several sights a day; for two people riding together it is cheaper still, because the price is per bike, not per person.</p>\n<h2>Simple ways to save in Da Lat</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Travel on weekdays and avoid holiday weekends if your dates are flexible.</li>\n<li>Book a stay a little outside the busiest streets and ride in.</li>\n<li>Have breakfast at a local stall, coffee at a local café, and save the view restaurant for one special evening.</li>\n<li>Carry cash in small notes: some stalls, parking lots and markets only take cash.</li>\n<li>Plan routes in loops so you don\'t ride back and forth — our <a href="{ROUTES}">routes page</a> helps.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Rent a scooter that fits your budget</h2>\n<p>One fixed price, no hidden fees, no cash deposit: that makes the transport part of your Da Lat budget easy. Choose your bike on the <a href="{HOME}">Da Lat motorbike rental page</a> and message us on WhatsApp, Zalo or Telegram. No alcohol when riding, and strictly follow Vietnamese traffic law.</p>\n')
C["articles"]['lt-checklist'] = ('what-to-check-before-renting-a-motorbike-da-lat', 'What to Check Before Renting a Motorbike in Da Lat: 10 Points | Van Phu', 'What to Check Before Renting a Motorbike in Da Lat: A 10-Point Checklist', 'What to check before renting a motorbike in Da Lat: brakes, tyres, lights, horn, mirrors, helmet, fuel and a video of the bike — a simple 10-point checklist.', "A few minutes of checking at pickup can save you a lot of trouble on Da Lat's hills. Here is a simple list of what to check before renting a motorbike in Da Lat — the same things we go through with every guest before they ride away.", 'honda-air-blade-vision-fleet-van-phu', '\n<h2>Why checking matters in Da Lat</h2>\n<p>Da Lat is a hilly city: long downhill streets, sharp bends, cool damp mornings and sudden rain. Brakes, tyres and lights work harder here than on flat roads. Whoever you rent from, take a few minutes before you leave. A good rental shop will be happy to go through it with you.</p>\n<h2>The 10-point checklist</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>1. Front and rear brakes.</b> Squeeze each lever; it should feel firm and stop the wheel without the lever touching the grip. Try them gently while rolling slowly.</li>\n<li><b>2. Tyres.</b> Look for visible tread, no cracks or bulges, and press to check they are not soft. Good tyres matter on wet, steep streets.</li>\n<li><b>3. Lights.</b> Headlight (low and high beam), tail light and brake light. Fog and early dark are common in Da Lat.</li>\n<li><b>4. Turn signals and horn.</b> You will use both all the time in Vietnamese traffic.</li>\n<li><b>5. Mirrors.</b> Both present, firm and adjusted for you before you start.</li>\n<li><b>6. Helmet.</b> It should fit snugly, the strap must click shut, and the visor (if any) should be clear. Ask for a different size if it moves.</li>\n<li><b>7. Fuel.</b> Look at the gauge and ask how much is in the tank so you know when to stop at a petrol station.</li>\n<li><b>8. Starting and throttle.</b> The engine should start easily and the throttle should spring back when you let go.</li>\n<li><b>9. Stand and seat.</b> The side or centre stand should hold the bike firmly; the seat should lock and the storage box should open.</li>\n<li><b>10. Video of the bike\'s condition.</b> Walk around the bike and film every side, including existing scratches, with the shop owner present. It protects both of you.</li>\n</ul>\n{PHOTO:guest-white-honda-vision-van-phu-shop}\n<h2>Also agree on the rules</h2>\n<p>Checking the bike is half of the job; the other half is knowing the terms. Before you sign or pay, make sure you understand the price per day, until what time the rental day lasts, what you leave instead of a deposit, and who to call if something goes wrong. Clear, written terms are a good sign. Our <a href="{ART:lt-procedure}">step-by-step rental guide</a> explains how it works with us.</p>\n<h2>How we do it at Van Phu</h2>\n<p>Before every rental we do a short test ride with you and film the bike\'s condition together. Every bike — Honda Vision 110cc, Honda Air Blade 125cc or electric scooter — costs 150,000 VND per day, the rental day runs from pickup until 21:00, and there is no cash deposit: you leave a passport or ID card. Helmets are free, every bike has a phone holder, and gas scooters come with 1 liter of fuel. Picking an electric scooter? It has a few extra points to look at — see <a href="{ART:lt-ev-checklist}">what to check when picking up an electric scooter</a>.</p>\n{PHOTO:air-blade-handover-dalat-homestay}\n<h2>On the road</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Vietnam drives on the right. Take the first few minutes slowly on quiet streets to get used to the bike.</li>\n<li>Use both brakes gently on long downhill roads.</li>\n<li>If anything feels wrong — a noise, soft brakes, a warning light — stop safely and message the shop.</li>\n<li>No alcohol or beer when riding, and strictly follow Vietnamese traffic law.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Ready to rent? See our bikes on the <a href="{HOME}">Da Lat motorbike rental page</a> and message us on WhatsApp, Zalo or Telegram — we\'ll have the bike checked and ready for you.</p>\n')
C["articles"]['lt-ev-checklist'] = ('electric-scooter-pickup-checklist-da-lat', 'What to Check When Picking Up an Electric Scooter in Da Lat | Van Phu', 'What to Check When Picking Up an Electric Scooter in Da Lat', 'Renting an electric scooter in Da Lat? What to check at pickup: both batteries, the display, brakes, lights, horn, power switch, helmet and a video of the scooter.', "An electric scooter is simple to ride, but it has a few things a petrol scooter doesn't — batteries, a display and a power switch. Here is what to check when picking up an electric scooter in Da Lat, so your ride around the city is calm and worry-free.", 'electric-scooters-van-phu-shop', '\n<h2>Batteries and display</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Two batteries.</b> Every Van Phu electric scooter comes with 2 batteries. Check that both are with the scooter and fully charged before you leave.</li>\n<li><b>Battery indicator.</b> Ask us to show you where the battery level appears on the display and how it looks when it is getting low.</li>\n<li><b>How to switch batteries.</b> Watch once how the second battery is used, so you are not guessing later on the road.</li>\n<li><b>Swap plan.</b> If you need more, come back to the shop and we swap in a full battery for 20,000 VND. How far the batteries go is explained in our <a href="{ART:lt-ev-battery}">battery guide</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Power, throttle and brakes</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Power on and off.</b> Learn how to turn the scooter on and off and how the key or switch works. An electric scooter makes almost no sound, so check the display to know it is on.</li>\n<li><b>Gentle throttle.</b> Electric motors pull straight away. Try the throttle slowly in the shop area before riding into traffic.</li>\n<li><b>Brakes.</b> Squeeze front and rear levers: they should feel firm and stop the wheels. Da Lat\'s streets are hilly even in the centre, so this matters.</li>\n<li><b>Tyres.</b> Visible tread, no cracks, not soft.</li>\n</ul>\n{PHOTO:guest-white-electric-scooter-dalat}\n<h2>Lights, horn and mirrors</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Headlight, tail light, brake light and turn signals all working.</li>\n<li><b>Horn:</b> test it. Because the motor is so quiet, pedestrians and other riders may not hear you coming — a short tap of the horn at junctions helps.</li>\n<li>Both mirrors present and adjusted for you.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Helmet, phone holder and storage</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>A free helmet for each rider that fits snugly, with the strap clicking shut.</li>\n<li>The phone holder is firm so you can follow the map without holding your phone.</li>\n<li>Check what fits in the storage space for a raincoat and water.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Video and rules before you ride</h2>\n<p>Before every rental we do a short test ride with you and film the scooter\'s condition together — every side, including existing scratches. Then we go through the main rules: maximum 2 riders with a combined weight under 140 kg, city and nearby sights only, and no long steep passes such as Prenn, Mimosa or Lang Biang — for those, choose the Honda Air Blade 125cc. The full list is in our <a href="{ART:evrules}">electric scooter rental rules</a>. Renting a petrol scooter instead? Use the <a href="{ART:lt-checklist}">10-point motorbike checklist</a>.</p>\n<h2>Rent an electric scooter in Da Lat</h2>\n<p>Our electric scooters cost 150,000 VND per day, like every bike, with no cash deposit — you leave a passport or ID card. The rental day runs from pickup until 21:00. Vietnam drives on the right; no alcohol when riding, and strictly follow Vietnamese traffic law. See the scooters on the <a href="{EV}">electric scooter rental page</a> or compare all bikes on the <a href="{HOME}">Da Lat motorbike rental page</a>, then message us on WhatsApp or Zalo.</p>\n')
