C["articles"]['passes'] = ('dalat-scenic-roads-viewpoints', 'Dalat Scenic Roads and Viewpoints by Motorbike: Passes & Safety | Van Phu', 'Dalat Scenic Roads and Viewpoints: Prenn, Mimosa and Other Beautiful Rides — Safely', 'Dalat scenic roads and viewpoints by motorbike: Prenn and Mimosa passes, the roads to Tuyen Lam, Lang Biang and Cau Dat, where to stop safely and how to ride mountain bends.', 'Da Lat sits on a plateau at about 1,500 metres, so almost every road out of town climbs, winds and opens onto pine valleys. Here are the most scenic roads and viewpoints around Da Lat — and how to ride them safely.', 'friends-air-blade-van-phu-gate', '\n<h2>Why the roads are half the trip</h2>\n<p>Around Da Lat the journey is often as beautiful as the destination: pine forests, valleys full of greenhouses, tea rows and, on good mornings, low clouds below the road. The flip side is that many of these roads are mountain roads — long descents, tight bends, trucks and sudden weather. With a little planning they are a joy to ride.</p>\n<h2>Prenn Pass — the main road south</h2>\n<p>Prenn Pass is the main road between Da Lat and Lien Khuong Airport, starting about 5–7 km south of the centre. It is wide and busy, with long curves through pine forest. Datanla Waterfall and Prenn Waterfall are along this road, and the Robin Hill cable car to Tuyen Lam Lake starts near the top. Expect buses and trucks at any hour: keep right, never overtake on a bend, and use the proper parking areas at the sights rather than stopping on the shoulder.</p>\n<h2>Mimosa Pass — winding and quieter</h2>\n<p>Mimosa Pass is the other, twistier road down from the plateau south-west of the city. It has tighter hairpins and steeper sections than Prenn, and in the rainy season parts of it are sometimes closed after landslides or for repairs. Ask us before you go — we\'ll tell you whether it\'s open and worth riding that week.</p>\n<h2>Easier scenic rides close to town</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Tuyen Lam Lake</b> (about 5–7 km south) — pine-lined roads around the lake and up to Truc Lam Monastery, with gentle viewpoints over the water. See our <a href="{ART:tuyenlam}">Tuyen Lam Lake guide</a>.</li>\n<li><b>Towards Lang Biang and Lac Duong</b> (about 10–12 km north) — open valleys and mountain views; the last climb to Radar Peak is by jeep or on foot.</li>\n<li><b>Trai Mat and the eastern villages</b> (about 8 km east) — quieter roads past flower farms and Linh Phuoc Pagoda.</li>\n<li><b>The road to the Cau Dat tea hills</b> (about 20–25 km east) — farmland, pine ridges and tea rows; best early in the morning.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>More loops with distances are on our page <a href="{ROUTES}">What to See in Da Lat by Motorbike</a>.</p>\n{PHOTO:van-phu-guest-air-blade-thumbs-up}\n<h2>Where to stop for photos</h2>\n<p>The best viewpoints are the ones with space to pull fully off the road: café terraces, temple and lake parking areas, village squares and wide lay-bys. Never stop on a bend, just after a crest or on the inside of a curve — drivers coming downhill can\'t see you. If you want a photo of the road itself, park first and walk back carefully on the edge.</p>\n<h2>How to ride a mountain pass safely</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Going down:</b> slow down before the bend, not in it. Use both brakes gently and evenly, and don\'t hold the brakes on all the way down — on long descents, stop at a safe spot for a few minutes to let them cool.</li>\n<li><b>Going up:</b> keep a steady throttle and stay well to the right; buses and trucks often need the whole lane in hairpins.</li>\n<li><b>Blind bends:</b> a short tap of the horn is normal in Vietnam and helps others know you\'re there.</li>\n<li><b>Weather:</b> fog can come in quickly in the morning and showers often arrive in the afternoon. Ride passes in the morning, turn your headlight on in fog, and keep a raincoat under the seat.</li>\n<li><b>Road surface:</b> watch for sand, gravel and red mud after rain, especially on the edges and in bends.</li>\n<li><b>Daylight:</b> plan to be back in town before dark; unlit mountain roads are much harder at night.</li>\n<li>No alcohol or beer when riding, and strictly follow Vietnamese traffic law. Vietnam drives on the right.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Which bike for scenic roads</h2>\n<p>For passes and longer climbs, take the Honda Air Blade 125cc: more power uphill, stable on descents and comfortable for two. The Honda Vision 110cc is fine for Tuyen Lam and the shorter rides near town. Our electric scooters are made for the city and nearby sights — please don\'t take them on long, steep passes such as Prenn, Mimosa or the road up Lang Biang. Read our <a href="{ART:riding}">motorbike tips for Vietnam</a> before your first long ride.</p>\n<h2>Rent a bike for your scenic ride</h2>\n<p>Every bike at Van Phu costs 150,000 VND per day, with no cash deposit — you leave your passport or ID card. A helmet and a phone holder come with every bike, and gas scooters come with 1 liter of fuel. Before you leave we do a test ride and film the bike\'s condition together. Message us on WhatsApp the evening before and we\'ll suggest the best scenic road for the weather and your experience.</p>\n')
C["articles"]['lt-rainy'] = ('motorbike-rental-da-lat-rainy-season', 'Renting a Motorbike in Da Lat in the Rainy Season: Tips | Van Phu', 'Renting a Motorbike in Da Lat in the Rainy Season: Dalat in the Rain — Transport Tips', 'Renting a motorbike in Da Lat in the rainy season: when it rains, what to wear, how to ride wet hills safely, what to avoid and which bike to choose. Dalat in the rain: transport tips.', "Rain is no reason to give up your own wheels in Da Lat — it just changes how you plan the day. Here's what you need to know about renting a motorbike in Da Lat in the rainy season, from timing your rides to braking on wet hills.", 'van-phu-shop-busy-morning-vision-air-blade', '\n<h2>What the rainy season is really like</h2>\n<p>Da Lat\'s rainy season runs roughly from May to October–November. It rarely rains all day: mornings are often dry and fresh, and heavy but fairly short showers tend to arrive in the afternoon. Some days stay grey and drizzly, and after heavy rain the hills stay wet for hours. So renting a motorbike in Da Lat in the rainy season works well — you simply ride the longer parts of your plan in the morning and keep the afternoon flexible.</p>\n<h2>Plan your day around the showers</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Leave early and do the out-of-town ride first; aim to be back in the centre by early afternoon.</li>\n<li>Keep a café, an indoor sight or the market as your "shower stop" — waiting 30–60 minutes is often all it takes.</li>\n<li>After a night of heavy rain, skip waterfalls with steep paths and long mountain passes; choose sights in town instead.</li>\n<li>Check the forecast the evening before, and message us — we see the local weather every morning.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Ideas for wet afternoons are in our article <a href="{ART:rainyday}">what to do in Da Lat on a rainy day</a>.</p>\n<h2>What to wear and bring</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>A proper raincoat or poncho (áo mưa) — they are sold cheaply in shops and markets all over town. Keep it under the seat.</li>\n<li>A warm layer: wet clothes plus wind on the bike make Da Lat feel cold.</li>\n<li>Shoes with grip, or sandals with straps — not loose flip-flops.</li>\n<li>A plastic bag or waterproof case for your phone and passport copy.</li>\n</ul>\n{PHOTO:honda-vision-bui-thi-xuan-street-dalat}\n<h2>Riding wet hills safely</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Double your braking distance and brake gently with both brakes, before bends rather than in them.</li>\n<li>Painted road markings, metal covers and smooth concrete get very slippery when wet; red mud and sand wash onto the edges of hill roads.</li>\n<li>Never ride through deep or flowing water — you can\'t see what\'s underneath.</li>\n<li>Turn your headlight on in rain and fog, ride slower than usual and keep more distance from the vehicle in front.</li>\n<li>If the rain becomes very heavy, pull off safely and wait under a roof — never under a tree in a storm.</li>\n<li>No alcohol or beer when riding, and strictly follow Vietnamese traffic law. Vietnam drives on the right.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Which bike in the rainy season?</h2>\n<p>The Honda Air Blade 125cc is the steadiest choice for hills and longer rides in the wet. The Honda Vision 110cc is light and easy in town. An electric scooter is fine for short city rides in light rain — read our tips on the <a href="{ART:lt-ev-rain}">electric scooter in the rain</a>. Whatever you choose, check the tyres and brakes during the test ride at pick-up; we film the bike\'s condition together before you leave.</p>\n<h2>Rent a motorbike in Da Lat, rain or shine</h2>\n<p>The price doesn\'t change with the weather: 150,000 VND per day for every bike, with no cash deposit — you leave your passport or ID card. A helmet and a phone holder are included, and gas scooters come with 1 liter of fuel. For rentals of 2 days or more we deliver free to hotels in central Da Lat, which saves you a wet walk to the shop. See all bikes on our <a href="{HOME}">Da Lat motorbike rental page</a> and message us on WhatsApp or Zalo with your dates.</p>\n')
C["articles"]['lt-ev-families'] = ('electric-scooter-da-lat-families-older-travellers', 'Electric Scooter Rental in Da Lat for Families & Older Travellers | Van Phu', 'Electric Scooter Rental in Da Lat for Families and Older Travellers: Gentle, Quiet and Safe', 'Electric scooter rental in Da Lat for families and older travellers: who it suits, the 2-rider and 140 kg rule, helmets for children, easy routes in the city, batteries and safety.', "Travelling with children or with parents who want an easy, calm ride? An electric scooter is light, quiet and has no gears. Here's how electric scooter rental in Da Lat works for families and older travellers — and when a different bike is the better choice.", 'electric-scooters-helmets-van-phu', '\n<h2>Why electric suits families and older riders</h2>\n<p>An electric scooter starts with a gentle twist of the throttle, has no gears and no clutch, and makes almost no noise. There is no hot exhaust pipe for a child\'s leg to touch, and the scooter is light to push, park and turn around. For relaxed rides between the hotel, Xuan Huong Lake, the flower garden and a café, many older travellers find it calmer than a petrol scooter.</p>\n<h2>The rules that matter for families</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Maximum 2 people per scooter, with a combined weight under 140 kg.</b> In practice that is one adult with a child, or two lighter adults. A family of four usually takes two scooters.</li>\n<li><b>Every rider wears a helmet</b>, passengers too. Free helmets come with every bike — try them on at the shop and make sure the strap is snug, especially for children.</li>\n<li><b>The rider must be at least 16.</b> A child always rides behind the adult, holding on with both hands, feet on the footrests.</li>\n<li>No alcohol or beer when riding, and strictly follow Vietnamese traffic law. Vietnam drives on the right.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>All rules are on one page: <a href="{ART:evrules}">electric scooter rental rules</a>.</p>\n{PHOTO:guest-white-electric-scooter-dalat}\n<h2>Easy family routes in the city</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Around Xuan Huong Lake, with stops at the city flower garden and Lam Vien Square.</li>\n<li>Da Lat Railway Station and Domaine de Marie church — short hops with parking nearby.</li>\n<li>Da Lat Market in the morning, then a café with a garden.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Choose short legs with breaks: children and older riders get tired and cold faster than they expect. Da Lat\'s streets are hilly even in the centre, so go slowly downhill and use both brakes gently. Avoid long, steep passes such as Prenn, Mimosa and the road up Lang Biang — for trips out of town, the Honda Air Blade 125cc is the better choice, at the same price.</p>\n<h2>Battery: simple to plan</h2>\n<p>Every scooter comes with 2 batteries. Hills and a passenger use more power, so head back when about a third is left. Need more? Come back to our central shop and we swap in a full battery for 20,000 VND — a good moment for a snack break. How far a battery goes is explained in our <a href="{ART:lt-ev-battery}">electric scooter battery guide</a>.</p>\n<h2>Tips for older travellers</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Take the free test ride at the shop and practise starting, stopping and turning in our quiet lane before you go.</li>\n<li>Ride in daylight, and plan to be back before dark.</li>\n<li>Dress warmly — mornings and evenings are cool on a scooter — and keep a raincoat under the seat.</li>\n<li>Pedestrians may not hear an electric scooter coming: ride slowly near markets and use the horn gently.</li>\n<li>Prefer not to ride? A passenger seat behind a family member works too.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Rent an electric scooter for your family</h2>\n<p>Each scooter costs 150,000 VND per day, with no cash deposit — you leave a passport or ID card. Helmets and a phone holder are included, and we film the scooter\'s condition together before you leave. For rentals of 2 days or more we deliver free to hotels in central Da Lat. See the scooters on our <a href="{EV}">electric scooter rental page</a> or compare all bikes on the <a href="{HOME}">Da Lat motorbike rental page</a>, then message us with the number of riders and we\'ll prepare the right bikes.</p>\n')
